Title: Possawatomie
Order: 6
Production Code: 1x06

INT. THE STATE PRISON, WARREN'S CELL - EVENING

PAGE: 1

PANEL: The next day, hot and sultry; signs of a thunder storm in the grey light of the cell window. Toward dark the cell door opens: the jailer OWENS with a lantern and, beside him, a young man in a city coat with a carpet bag, in a great hurry to catch a train.

CAPTION: The next morning dawned hot and sultry; all day there were signs of a thunder storm.

PANEL: The visitor, at the grated door, talking to the jailer and pointing out some means of ventilation outside. One hand behind his back.

VISITOR
I have been requested by the British consul at New York, who has heard of your case, to see you.

VISITOR (CONT'D)
All will be done that can be done.

PANEL: Close on the visitor's hand behind his back: a small slip of paper, held out to the bars. Warren's hand takes it.

PANEL: When they are gone: Warren alone, unfolding the slip with trembling fingers.

PANEL: Close on the slip of paper.

CAPTION: Be ready at midnight.

PAGE: 2

PANEL: Footsteps and voices again in the passage; the sallow, uncanny face of BILL THOMSON framed in the doorway, hat tilted.

THOMSON
Had a visitor sent by the British consul, did he? Well, that won't save his neck.

PANEL: Thomson leans on the grating, smiling.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
I tell you, Bub, saltpetre won't save you. You've got to go, by G--d.

PANEL: Night. Warren on the cot, awake, watching the window as the shades of dark come down. A clock in the corridor tolling.

PANEL: The storm breaking; rain hammering the window; far off, the river swollen, a mimic Niagara; Warren thinking over the three storms of his American adventures.

CAPTION: The storms had attended three critical periods in his history while in America.

PANEL: The clock strikes twelve. A thundering knock from the outer door.

SFX: BANG BANG BANG

PAGE: 3

INT. THE STATE PRISON, THE CORRIDOR - NIGHT

PANEL: Owens at the outer door with a lantern, his face to the crack; voices outside in the rain.

OWENS
What do you want?

OUTSIDE (O.P.)
We are from Andrew County, with a prisoner we want put in jail for safe keeping. A notorious horse thief.

PANEL: Owens, suspicious.

OWENS
Have you a warrant?

OUTSIDE (O.P.)
No; but it's all right. We'll satisfy you in the morning.

PANEL: Owens lets them in and the light falls on their faces: MAYBEE, JOHN BROWN, and a slight dark youth between them with a hat low over his eyes and wrists bound.

PANEL: Owens starts back with a cry.

OWENS (CONT'D)
It's Allen Pinks!

PANEL: Brown, grim, with a pistol out and level.

BROWN
Yes, Mr. Owens, it's the boy, and it's too late to make a noise.

BROWN (CONT'D)
If you resist or give an alarm, you are a dead man.

PAGE: 4

PANEL: In his cell, Warren watches between the bars of the cell door; behind the group, a dark object moves along the corridor wall.

PANEL: A door opens far down the row. THOMSON, fully dressed, pistol in hand, steps into the light.

PANEL: Thomson advances step by step with his eyes fixed on the negro lad. The boy involuntarily cries out and covers his face with his hands.

PANEL: Thomson, with a long cold grin, tilting his pistol.

THOMSON
Well, sir! If it ain't Winona!

PANEL: Close on Thomson turning his head to the jailer, flat and amused.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
Looks interestin', Owens, that you couldn't tell a gal dressed up in boys clo's. This strikes me heavy.

PAGE: 5 (TURN)

PANEL: Warren in his cell, standing helpless with his hands on the bars; the light dawning in his face; every puzzle falling into place.

CAPTION: Warren saw and heard all, and understood many things that had puzzled him.

PANEL: Winona, hat fallen, the cropped head bare, a stain on her skin, face lifted; fearless now; the boy's coat torn open at the collar.

PANEL: Brown's pistol up, Thomson's aimed at Winona's breast.

BROWN
Halt! Halt, or you're a dead man!

THOMSON
I fire if you come a step nearer.

PANEL: Warren at the bars, fists clenched.

WARREN
Oh, for a moment's freedom and a good weapon in my hand!

PANEL: Behind Thomson, in the shadow of the corridor wall, a huge shape crouched almost double; a lurid glare from the lantern on one dark shoulder.

PAGE: 6

PANEL: Warren's cry rings down the passage.

WARREN
It is Judah! It is Judah!

PANEL: A huge leap from the dark; Judah seizing Thomson in an iron grasp and dragging him backward to the ground with such violence that the pistol flies from his hand.

SFX: WHUMP

PANEL: Judah's foot on Thomson's chest, Thomson's pistol in Judah's hand, aimed at his face.

JUDAH
It is between you and me, now. Our roles are reversed. It is you who must die.

PANEL: Brown comes forward, hand up.

BROWN
No, no; it won't do. Spare him!

PANEL: Judah, without taking his eyes off Thomson.

JUDAH
Spare him! For what? To afford him an opportunity to do more mischief? No, no!

PAGE: 7

PANEL: Close on Judah's face, burning.

JUDAH
If I do not kill this man, he will murder me by inches if I fall into his hands.

JUDAH (CONT'D)
I hate him, I hate him!

PANEL: Judah stoops and places the pistol barrel on Thomson's forehead; Thomson's eyes huge.

PANEL: Brown throws himself on the enraged black and stays his hand; both gripping the pistol.

PANEL: Brown, quiet, close.

BROWN
Don't do it; not this time, Judah. There's too much at stake now. We owe Mr. Maxwell something for all he has suffered.

PANEL: Maybee at Brown's side, rolling up his sleeves.

MAYBEE
If you let up now, Judah, I'll be tee-totally smashed if I don't lend you a hand and stand by for fair play.

PAGE: 8

PANEL: Judah's face turned to Brown, his eyes dark and glowing.

JUDAH
Why stay my hand? Vengeance is sweet.

BROWN
There is a time for everything, my son. Stay thy hand and fear not; vengeance is mine.

PANEL: Judah, standing still, gathering strength; his hands tremble; he lowers the pistol.

PANEL: Judah, hard, to the two men.

JUDAH
I am the Lord's instrument to kill this man.

JUDAH (CONT'D)
Promise me that when this villain's life shall lie in the gift of any man in the camp, he shall be given to me.

PANEL: Brown and Maybee, side by side, two hands raised.

BROWN
We promise.

MAYBEE
We promise.

PANEL: The trio, with Winona in her boy's clothes, binding Owens and the other jail officials with cords and pushing them into Warren's abandoned cell with Thomson, hands and feet tied; the slaves in the lower room crying out, begging.

CAPTION: It was hard to resist the entreaties of the slaves confined there, but it was deemed advisable not to burden themselves with fugitive slaves.

PAGE: 9

PANEL: Maybee unlocks the cell door with the warden's key; the lantern shows Warren: a wreck, a sling on his arm, red scars, face disfigured by burns.

CAPTION: There were tears in his eyes as he beheld the wreck that two months of imprisonment had made of the stalwart athlete.

PANEL: Maybee, folding the emaciated form in his arms, openly crying.

MAYBEE
This, my son, was dead and is alive again.

PANEL: Maybee wiping his eyes with a fist, embarrassed.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
Oh, never m-min' my cryin'! 'Taint nothin'. Some fellers cries easier than others.

PANEL: Warren holds each of their hands; Winona sobbing; Judah turning his head with his own eyes wet.

WARREN
I never thought to see you again, boys. I understand the slavery question through and through. Experience is a stern teacher.

PANEL: Maybee, wiping his nose.

MAYBEE
If you don' fin' Bill Thomson somewhere, some time, an' choke him an' tear his win'pipe to fiddlestrings,

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
you ain't got a drop of British blood in yer whole carcass!

PAGE: 10

PANEL: Judah lifts Warren in his strong arms and carries him down the corridor; Winona in her boy's clothes walks by his side with her eyes on the floor.

CAPTION: He felt all his passionate jealousy die a sudden death as pity stirred his heart for the sufferings of his rival.

PANEL: Brown takes the keys; he locks the cell door on the officials and the prisoner Thomson; the great outer door pulled shut; both keys in his coat.

PANEL: Outside, in the lashing rain, ten stalwart Free-State men waiting in the dark with their horses; a wagon.

PANEL: Warren in the straw in the bottom of the cart, rain on his face, staring at Winona on the seat beside the driver; he sees the stain on her skin and the lineaments beneath.

CAPTION: He wondered at his own stupidity in not recognizing Winona.

PANEL: The wagon and the riders at a gallop toward the river at first light; the storm clearing in the east.

PAGE: 11

EXT. THE MISSOURI RIVER - DAWN

PANEL: The river bank at dawn. Warren lifted into a boat; strong arms propelling it toward the Kansas shore; the wagon and horses returned to the friendly settlers who had loaned them.

PANEL: On the Kansas side, fresh horses and another wagon; settlers turning out to cheer along a farm lane.

CAPTION: Maxwell's story was known in every village and town throughout the country.

PANEL: A farmhouse kitchen: a hot bath, a basin of water, clean coarse clothes laid out; a farm wife handing Warren a bowl.

PANEL: Travelling all that day and night by fresh horses at intervals, the party strung out; hay-stacks like monuments in twilight, any of which might conceal foes.

PANEL: Afternoon: the pleasant hollow two miles from camp; the great hills ahead; Judah lifting Warren from the wagon at the camp's gate.

PAGE: 12

EXT. BROWN'S CAMP - DAY (WEEKS LATER)

PANEL: The camp. A week after his rescue Warren is up, gaunt and sunburned, carrying wood with the others; Winona at a cook-fire, her hair growing out in short curls, not looking at him.

CAPTION: A week after his rescue found him up and about gradually joining in the duties of the camp.

PANEL: Brown and Warren side by side over a table of sketches: maps of old battlefields with positions marked in a neat hand.

CAPTION: Captain Brown was familiar with all the great battles of ancient and modern times.

PANEL: Brown with his finger on a map, eyes alight, Warren listening.

BROWN
There must be an uprising of such magnitude that it should once and for all time settle the question of slavery.

PANEL: Warren, heavy at heart, one hand over the other.

WARREN
I will give money and ammunition and arms.

PANEL: Maybee joking with Warren at the cabin door.

MAYBEE
You want to go home? We'll start to-morrow morning on foot.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
Or this evenin'; the Rangers are all over the place between here an' civilization.

PAGE: 13

PANEL: Maybee running in out of breath, the next afternoon.

MAYBEE
Well, young feller, we're in fer it, an' no mistake. You'll git fightin' a-plenty before forty-eight hours.

PANEL: Maybee, grinning wolfishly.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
The Rangers are at Carlton's. Reynolds has come up to camp an' brought Brown the news.

PANEL: Warren, quite calm.

WARREN
I shall not feel safe until I get my feet on British soil once more.

WARREN (CONT'D)
I'll never set foot on the soil of the "greatest Republic on earth" again.

PANEL: Maybee, delivering a message with a flourish.

MAYBEE
Reynolds left you a message. Thomson says the nex' time he gits you he'll flog you first like a nigger, an' then burn you.

PANEL: Maybee, solemn.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
An' send your ashes to your folks in England in a chiny vase. How's that strike you?

PAGE: 14

PANEL: The camp in a hurry: men carrying sacks and boxes up a hillside to a cave mouth; women carrying rifles; Brown directing.

CAPTION: Winona's cave on the mountainside was to be stored with provision, ammunition and all other necessaries.

PANEL: Warren climbing to the highest peak and settling himself as lookout; the plain spread out far beneath; the Possawatomie a silver thread.

PANEL: Winona climbing up the hillside with downcast eyes; Warren sees her and calls.

WARREN
Come, see this fine sweep of country. We cannot be surprised.

PANEL: She sits on a jagged rock overhanging the cliff; the wind in the pines; neither looks at the other.

WINONA
How a man glories in war.

WARREN
You, with your Indian training, ought to feel with us and not think of fear.

PANEL: Winona, looking out at the plain, proud and hurt.

WINONA
But then, I am not of the blood.

PAGE: 15

PANEL: Winona, with a suspicion in her voice that she cannot hide.

WINONA
Better an Indian than a Negro? I do not blame you for your preference.

PANEL: Warren, hurt, a step toward her.

WARREN
Is it possible that you can think so meanly of me?

PANEL: Winona, humbly, in the language of the tribes.

WINONA
Are you not a white brave? Do not all of them hate the black blood?

PANEL: Warren, gently, very firmly.

WARREN
No; not all white men, thank God.

WARREN (CONT'D)
In my country we think not of the color of the skin but of the man, the woman, the heart.

PANEL: Winona, her head against a tree, a cloud of pine ash on her knees.

WINONA
Which is my country, I wonder? Judah says that he will not fight for the Stars and Stripes if war comes.

PANEL: Winona, quietly.

WINONA (CONT'D)
My father's country shall be mine.

PAGE: 16

PANEL: Warren, amused, almost teasing.

WARREN
Better reserve your decision until you marry.

WINONA
I shall never marry.

PANEL: Winona, head proudly erect.

WINONA (CONT'D)
I cannot marry out of the class of my father. It follows, then, that I shall never marry.

PANEL: Warren, protesting, kind.

WARREN
Nonsense. You will not live and grow old alone. Mere birth does not count for more than one's whole training afterward.

PANEL: Winona, with a smile on her lips and gravity in her eyes.

WINONA
The degradation of the two years just passed can never leave me. I shall be a nun.

PANEL: Warren, with a tear under his smile.

WARREN
How can you talk of convents, you who will go to England with me? Perhaps, who can tell, you may marry a duke.

PAGE: 17 (TURN)

PANEL: Silence; the evening shadows gradually shutting the eye of day. Two figures on a rock.

PANEL: A bugle's three notes carried over the hills; Winona springs to her feet.

SFX: TAA-RAA-RAAA

WINONA
'Tis Judah! There is danger! Let us go at once!

PANEL: Her foot goes out over the edge of the cliff; a sheer drop to the Possawatomie below.

PANEL: Warren catches her round the waist just in time; she clings to him in fright.

WARREN
God!

PANEL: Warren's cheek against her cropped curly head.

WARREN (CONT'D)
Winona, let me say it now before we go to meet we know not what.

WARREN (CONT'D)
Thank God I have known you, so noble, so patient, so sweet.

PAGE: 18

PANEL: Full-width: a tall rock; against the sky the two figures in one embrace, the plain behind them gone to dusk, the first star out.

CAPTION: Forgetful of time and place, youth yielded to the sway of the love-god, and for one dazzling instant the glory of heaven shone upon them.

PANEL: Close on Winona's closed eyes and her thought.

CAPTION: What harm just once?
